{"qid":"Q250515","names":["Eastern Ganga Kingdom"],"title":"Eastern Ganga Kingdom","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Eastern Ganga dynasty ruled Kalinga (modern Odisha and northern Andhra Pradesh) from the mid-eleventh century, with Anantavarman Chodaganga (r. c. 1078–1148) uniting the territory and commissioning the Jagannath Temple at Puri around 1135, which became the dynasty's paramount religious monument. Narasimhadeva I (r. 1238–1264) built the Konark Sun Temple, one of the supreme achievements of medieval Indian temple architecture, and repulsed multiple Mamluk cavalry incursions into Odisha around 1247–1256. The dynasty weakened through succession conflict after the late thirteenth century and was effectively supplanted by the Gajapati rulers by the mid-fifteenth century, though the Eastern Ganga line formally persisted until the mid-fourteenth century.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Eastern Ganga core territory in coastal Odisha is attested by copper-plate grants and temple inscriptions, but the northern and southern extents of their polity are contested in secondary scholarship.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Eastern Ganga dynasty","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastern_Ganga_dynasty"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Anantavarman Chodaganga","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anantavarman_Chodaganga"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Konark Sun Temple","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Konark_Sun_Temple"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Eastern Ganga dynasty","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Indravarman I","nativeName":"ఇంద్రవర్మ I","roman":"Indravarman I","start":498,"end":537,"